{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"9d3a71c311119fd7","name":"card_event_volunteer_corps_home","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Japan/Homefront/events/card_event_volunteer_corps_home","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"VOLUNTEER CORPS","text":"Remove a unit from the battlefield until the end of turn.","flavorText":"In 1945, with defeat looming, Japan mobilized its civilian population in an unprecedented effort to resist an expected Allied invasion. This last-ditch strategy led to the formation of the Volunteer Fighting Corps, or Kokumin Giyū Sentōtai. It was the Japanese equivalent of Germany’s Volkssturm, intended to bolster homeland defense through civilian militias. Initially established in March 1945 as unarmed civil defense units focused on firefighting and evacuation, the corps soon evolved into a militarized force. By June, a new law authorized prefectural governors to conscript nearly all able-bodied men aged 15 to 60 and unmarried women aged 17 to 40. Though training and weapons were often scarce, some units practiced basic combat and fulfilled support roles like building fortifications and transporting supplies. Commanders were usually retired officers or civilians with military experience. These hastily assembled militias never saw combat due to Japan's surrender in August 1945. Still, their formation reflected the desperate state of the empire in its final months and the extent to which it was prepared to sacrifice its civilian population in defense of the home islands.","faction":"Japan","type":"order","rarity":"Common","cost":1,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"GameplayTags":["ability.bond"],"Name":"card_event_volunteer_corps_home","title":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_event_volunteer_corps_home_title","SourceString":"VOLUNTEER CORPS","LocalizedString":"VOLUNTEER CORPS"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Japan","kredits":1,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_event_volunteer_corps_home_text","SourceString":"Remove a unit from the battlefield until the end of turn.","LocalizedString":"Remove a unit from the battlefield until the end of turn."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_event_volunteer_corps_home_history","SourceString":"In 1945, with defeat looming, Japan mobilized its civilian population in an unprecedented effort to resist an expected Allied invasion. This last-ditch strategy led to the formation of the Volunteer Fighting Corps, or Kokumin Giyū Sentōtai. It was the Japanese equivalent of Germany’s Volkssturm, intended to bolster homeland defense through civilian militias. Initially established in March 1945 as unarmed civil defense units focused on firefighting and evacuation, the corps soon evolved into a militarized force. By June, a new law authorized prefectural governors to conscript nearly all able-bodied men aged 15 to 60 and unmarried women aged 17 to 40. Though training and weapons were often scarce, some units practiced basic combat and fulfilled support roles like building fortifications and transporting supplies. Commanders were usually retired officers or civilians with military experience. These hastily assembled militias never saw combat due to Japan's surrender in August 1945. Still, their formation reflected the desperate state of the empire in its final months and the extent to which it was prepared to sacrifice its civilian population in defense of the home islands.","LocalizedString":"In 1945, with defeat looming, Japan mobilized its civilian population in an unprecedented effort to resist an expected Allied invasion. This last-ditch strategy led to the formation of the Volunteer Fighting Corps, or Kokumin Giyū Sentōtai. It was the Japanese equivalent of Germany’s Volkssturm, intended to bolster homeland defense through civilian militias. Initially established in March 1945 as unarmed civil defense units focused on firefighting and evacuation, the corps soon evolved into a militarized force. By June, a new law authorized prefectural governors to conscript nearly all able-bodied men aged 15 to 60 and unmarried women aged 17 to 40. Though training and weapons were often scarce, some units practiced basic combat and fulfilled support roles like building fortifications and transporting supplies. Commanders were usually retired officers or civilians with military experience. These hastily assembled militias never saw combat due to Japan's surrender in August 1945. Still, their formation reflected the desperate state of the empire in its final months and the extent to which it was prepared to sacrifice its civilian population in defense of the home islands."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Common","selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":true,"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::Homefront","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnEndOfTurn"],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Taiko_Hits_2'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Taiko_Hits_2.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Japan/10_Homefront/T_volunteer_corps.T_volunteer_corps","SubPathString":""},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/VOLUNTEER_CORPS.VOLUNTEER_CORPS","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":373.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_event_volunteer_corps_home_photo","SourceString":"Japanese female students learning gun handling. Japan, 1945.","LocalizedString":"Japanese female students learning gun handling. Japan, 1945."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""},"isRemoval":true},"variables":[],"functions":[{"name":"CanPlayFromHand","owner":"card_event_volunteer_corps_home_C","params":[{"name":"canIt","type":"bool","direction":"out"},{"name":"reason","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"reasonParam1","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":